The theorem being applied
-------------------------

Let the named inner group G be perfect and transitive on Omega. Nonabelian
simplicity is a sufficient, stronger accepted premise in the application.
Let empty != T != Omega and F={T,T^a}. Suppose both actual endpoints a,c
stabilize this unordered family. Put K=Stab_G(F). If K=G, the induced action
G -> Sym(F) <= S2 is trivial because G is perfect. Then G preserves T,
contradicting transitivity. Thus K is proper. Any actual middle b in K has
<a,b,c> <= K < G. This also works when F is a singleton, and when its union
is the whole point domain. It does not require intransitivity of that union.

In the retained invariant-subset construction T is fixed by <ab,bc>.
Since ac=(ab)(bc), a normalizes <ab,ac>, and F is invariant under a,b,c.
The new pair role does not repeat those rotation checks: it directly verifies
both endpoint actions on F. The old strict subset result supplies a useful
seed with exact provenance, not an unchecked endpoint premise.

If each of d_x,d_y,d_z stabilizes F, their ordered product does too, since K
is a subgroup. When |F|=2 its fix/swap bit is the XOR of the three bits.
Both parities belong to K. No commutativity or XOR multiplication in the
Original D-family is assumed. Failure of a factor to stabilize F does not
establish generation, and it does not exclude a product whose factors cancel
their actions on F. Such rows remain unknown to this sufficient criterion.

These are elementary group-action arguments already present in the retained
